[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Tailwind CSS: Tree-shaking, Content Detection, and Avoiding Purge Pitfalls"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tailwind CSS relies on a build-time process to scan your template files and generate only the CSS classes you actually use in your project. This process, often referred to as tree-shaking or purging, is essential for keeping your production CSS bundle lightweight. However, improper configuration or dynamic class generation can lead to missing styles in production."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Content Configuration"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Tailwind v3 and later, the 'content' array in your tailwind.config.js file tells the compiler where to look for class names. If a file is omitted from this array, Tailwind will not detect any classes used within it, resulting in unstyled elements."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  content: [
    "./index.html",
    "./src/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx,vue}",
  ],
  theme: {
    extend: {},
  },
  plugins: [],
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Pitfall of Dynamic Class Names"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tailwind's scanner uses a simple regular expression to extract class strings from your source files. It does not evaluate JavaScript or interpret logic. This means string concatenation or dynamic interpolation will fail to be detected by the compiler."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// DANGEROUS: Tailwind will NOT detect this class in production
const color = 'red';
return <div className={`text-${color}-500`}>Warning</div>;"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"How to Safely Handle Dynamic Styles"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always write out full class names so the scanner can detect them easily."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Map dynamic values to static, complete class strings using objects or lookup maps."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// SAFE: Full class names are explicitly present in the source code
const colorMap = {
  red: "text-red-500",
  blue: "text-blue-500",
  green: "text-green-500"
};

return <div className={colorMap[color]}>Success</div>;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Safelist and Blocklist Configuration"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you must generate classes dynamically and cannot use a lookup map, you can use the 'safelist' option in your tailwind.config.js file to explicitly force Tailwind to include specific classes or patterns."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  content: ["./src/**/*.{html,js}"],
  safelist: [
    'bg-red-500',
    'bg-blue-500',
    {
      pattern: /bg-(red|green|blue)-(100|500|900)/,
    },
  ],
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Proper content detection guarantees high performance through optimal tree-shaking while preventing frustrating production bugs. Always prioritize fully written class names, use lookup maps for dynamic variants, and reserve safelists as a last resort."}]
